FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:2698-2713 — `first_invalid_base64_byte` whitelists `=` as always valid, then line 2713 unconditionally replaces the crate's `InvalidByte` position with the first character outside `[A-Za-z0-9+/=]`. A misplaced `=` is itself the first refusal, so for input like `"aG=k!"` the crate reports the offending byte at the `=` (position 2/3) while the scan points at the later `!` (position 4). That contradicts the function's own contract ("first byte that made it refuse", lines 2692-2693) and `SPEC.md:1553` ("naming the offending position"). This is an error-message regression, not a panic. Fix: treat `=` as valid only in a trailing padding run, or apply the override only when `first_invalid_base64_byte(text) < crate_position` (take the minimum), so a responsible `=` is never masked by a later bad byte. VERIFIED: traced lines 2698-2704 and the unconditional override at 2713; not executed.

2. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:2719-2730 — the `InvalidLastSymbol`, `InvalidLength` and `InvalidPadding` arms are user-reachable but have no test anywhere. The new suites only pin `InvalidByte` (`"aGk*"`, `"aGk!!"`, `"é"`) and hex odd-length; there is no case for a non-canonical final symbol (`base64.decode("aG")`), an incomplete group (`base64.decode("aGkAA")`), or bad padding, yet the REPORT's edge-case matrix at `phases/phase-104/REPORT.md:69` claims malformed input is fully covered. If these arms are truly unreachable they are dead code; if reachable, three user-facing message paths are unverified. Add one test per arm; also note the `InvalidPadding` message names the *last* `=` via `rfind`, not the first misplaced one.

3. [MINOR] tests/encoding_hashing_test.rs:249-259 and tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:1268-1271 — the SPEC promise at `SPEC.md:1580` that `uuid.generate()` returns a *version-4* UUID in *canonical lowercase* is never asserted. The existing tests only check that the value parses back, is 36 characters, and differs between draws; the docs test only checks `length(...) == 36`. A regression returning an unversioned 36-character UUID (or wrong case) would pass every test. Add a program/test that asserts the version nibble (`^........-....-4...-[89ab]...`) or the RFC-4122 variant bits.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
